1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,320
This morning, we're going to do a liturgical prayer for Mother's Day.

2
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:12,320
And there is a congregational part that you're going to play.

3
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:16,240
So when the words are bolded, that's your part.

4
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:18,420
All right, so we'll start with that.

5
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:23,280
Lord, on this day, set aside to honor and remember mothers.

6
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:25,380
We give you thanks for our mothers.

7
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:29,120
We are grateful that you chose to give us life through them

8
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:33,520
and that they received the gift of life from your hands and gave it to us.

9
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:38,560
Thank you for the sacrifices that they made in carrying us and giving us birth.

10
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:43,460
We thank you for the women who raised us and who were our mothers in childhood,

11
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:52,060
whether birth mom, adopted mom, older sister, aunt, grandmother, stepmother, or someone else.

12
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:57,080
We thank you for these women who have held us, fed us, and cared for us.

13
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:01,280
We pray that our lives may reflect the love that they showed us

14
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:04,480
and that they would be pleased to be called our moms.

15
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:08,860
We pray for the older moms whose children are grown.

16
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:14,340
We pray for the older moms and their children are grown.

17
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:17,980
We pray for new moms experiencing changes they could not predict.

18
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:22,800
We pray for their breasts in peace since they trust you with the age of children.

19
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,400
We pray for pregnant women who will soon be moms.

20
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:30,400
We pray for the patience and good counsel of the young moms.

21
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,780
We pray for moms who face the demands of single parenthood.

22
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:38,080
We pray for the strength and good counsel of the youth.

23
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:43,420
We pray for moms who enjoy financial abundance.

24
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:46,500
We pray for the time to share with their families.

25
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:50,180
We pray for moms who are raising their children in poverty.

26
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:52,940
We pray for their relief and justice.

27
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:55,540
We pray for stepmoms.

28
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:59,140
We pray for the mutations come to shame in the heart.

29
00:01:59,140 --> 00:02:02,600
We pray for moms who are separated from their children.

30
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,980
We pray for the faith and hope.

31
00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:09,180
We pray for moms in marriages that are in crisis.

32
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:12,060
We pray for the support and the sight.

33
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:15,480
We pray for moms who have lost children.

34
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:23,000
We pray for mothers who aborted their children.

35
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,560
We pray for the comfort and healing.

36
00:02:25,560 --> 00:02:29,440
We pray for moms who chose adoption for their children.

37
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:32,740
We pray for peace as they trust your problems.

38
00:02:32,740 --> 00:02:35,960
We pray for adaptive mothers.

39
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:40,160
We pray for the joy and clarity that we give to the child.

40
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:44,340
We pray for girls and women who think about being moms.

41
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:47,040
We pray for wisdom and discernment.

42
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:51,580
We pray for women longing to be mothers and yet unable.

43
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:55,440
We pray for encouragement and contentment in the waiting.

44
00:02:55,440 --> 00:03:00,460
We pray for all women who have assumed the mother's role in a child's life.

45
00:03:00,460 --> 00:03:03,980
We pray for joy and appreciation for their children.

46
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:09,500
We pray for those people present who are grieving the loss of their mother.

47
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:13,280
We pray for comfort and hope for Christ's resurrection.

48
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:16,000
Thank you.

49
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Thank you.

50
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:40,500
All my life you have been so, so good.

51
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:46,420
Every breath that I have made.

52
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:54,900
Oh, I will sing of the goodness of God.

53
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:55,680
One more time.

54
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:55,780
One more time.

55
00:03:55,780 --> 00:03:56,480
All my life.

56
00:03:56,480 --> 00:04:14,560
All my life you have been so, so good.

57
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:20,460
Every breath that I have made.

58
00:04:22,460 --> 00:04:28,940
I will sing of the goodness of God.

59
00:04:28,940 --> 00:04:35,820
The goodness of the goodness.

60
00:04:52,460 --> 00:05:19,000
Well, good morning.

61
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:22,020
Happy Mother's Day to everyone.

62
00:05:22,020 --> 00:05:24,420
So grateful that you've chosen to join us.

63
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:28,680
Happy Mother's Day to my mother and my wife.

64
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:31,340
This is her second Mother's Day, which is exciting.

65
00:05:31,340 --> 00:05:35,700
You know, the biggest fear of mine as a pastor used to be coming up here with my fly down,

66
00:05:35,700 --> 00:05:36,920
if I'm being totally honest.

67
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:40,420
I used to have nightmares about that.

68
00:05:40,420 --> 00:05:45,200
And I was about 30 seconds away from walking up here with a pair of bluey sunglasses on my head.

69
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:51,020
So that might be my new, you know, with parenting comes new nightmares.

70
00:05:51,020 --> 00:05:53,820
And so that was, that was almost, that almost happened.

71
00:05:53,820 --> 00:05:58,300
Hey, I just want to say, if you're new here, you haven't met me yet, my name is Ryan.

72
00:05:58,300 --> 00:06:00,940
I have the privilege of being the lead pastor here.

73
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:08,760
And I am just really so honored to have the opportunity to be in this role.

74
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:10,720
And I just wanted to take some time.

75
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:15,800
I've had a couple weeks off from preaching and it's given me time to reflect and think about how grateful I am for our church.

76
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:18,380
How grateful I am for all of you.

77
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:23,480
My wife and I have been a part of many churches and been on staff and been volunteers.

78
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:26,480
And there's something really special about this place.

79
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:28,520
We're not perfect by any stretch of the imagination.

80
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:36,300
But we are so grateful to call you guys family and for all the ways in which you bless us and encourage us.

81
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:38,880
And so I just wanted to take just a quick moment to say thank you.

82
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:42,940
You know, there's a reason why I don't preach every single week.

83
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:49,600
We talk about this a lot and it's that we don't want the church to ever be built on a person because the church is all of us.

84
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:54,940
And so I never, whenever I talk to people about how great our church is, I never call it my church.

85
00:06:54,940 --> 00:06:58,420
I always call it our church because I believe that with my whole heart.

86
00:06:58,420 --> 00:07:04,720
And if you are new, this is my shameless plug slash invitation to our Canvas Connection class.

87
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:08,400
I know we already talked about it, but it's next week and the week after.

88
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:09,920
I'll be leading it right after service.

89
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:12,520
And I'd love for you to just come and learn a little bit more about our church.

90
00:07:12,520 --> 00:07:14,220
You don't have to become a member at the end of it.

91
00:07:14,220 --> 00:07:16,080
If you're like, membership sounds weird.

92
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:19,440
And you told me I had to park farther if I became a member and I don't like doing that.

93
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:19,960
That's fine.

94
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:25,100
But I think it's important that you learn why we do things the way that we do things.

95
00:07:25,100 --> 00:07:26,420
What's really important to us.

96
00:07:26,420 --> 00:07:32,520
I can't tell you the amount of times I've had conversations with people who have been coming to our church for six months and thought they loved it.

97
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:36,420
And then they learn some stuff about our church and they're like, you think what?

98
00:07:36,420 --> 00:07:37,980
You believe what?

99
00:07:37,980 --> 00:07:38,900
And then they leave mad.

100
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:43,720
And I'm like, I would have just come to me week one and ask me and we could have avoided a lot of problems.

101
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:50,160
And so I'd love for you to come to that and just learn a little bit more about our church because we love it and we want you to love it.

102
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:54,040
And if you don't love it, we promise that there's another good church in our city that you will love.

103
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:57,800
And so we want that to be your experience if your experience isn't loving ours.

104
00:07:57,800 --> 00:07:58,440
Does that make sense?

105
00:07:59,340 --> 00:08:00,180
Okay, cool.

106
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:01,200
So I'd love for you to come.

107
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:04,320
Today we're launching a brand new series.

108
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:07,520
It's a summer series that we are calling A Summer's Journey.

109
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:12,880
We're going through the life and ministry of the Apostle Paul.

110
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:19,160
And I just want to give you like a peek behind the curtain into how we formulate our sermon series here at Canvas.

111
00:08:20,220 --> 00:08:25,480
We try really hard to balance kind of both practical and exegetical.

112
00:08:25,480 --> 00:08:29,700
So we want the messages to have practical implications for your everyday life, right?

113
00:08:29,700 --> 00:08:35,120
Like we want you to know the ways in which Jesus impacts the way that you live on Monday through Saturday.

114
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:36,520
It's important to us.

115
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:42,280
But we also want to preach verse by verse through the Bible as much as possible because we believe in the authority of Scripture.

116
00:08:42,460 --> 00:08:45,960
And so there's a challenging, delicate balance there.

117
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:47,580
So we try really hard to do that.

118
00:08:47,580 --> 00:08:53,440
We try really hard to have messages that are both applicable to the mature follower of Jesus.

119
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:54,460
Some of you have been in here.

120
00:08:54,460 --> 00:08:56,200
You've been following Jesus for 50 years.

121
00:08:56,200 --> 00:09:01,380
I want the messages that we share to challenge you, to encourage you, to give you hope and peace.

122
00:09:01,380 --> 00:09:06,820
And we know that there are people in this room that aren't yet followers of Jesus that are just curious about this thing.

123
00:09:06,820 --> 00:09:09,840
You've got a neighbor who acts really strange, but you like them.

124
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:12,160
And so you're like, we'll see what's going on here.

125
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:16,600
Or Michelle gives your kids lots of candy and they want you to come more than you want to come.

126
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:24,420
And so we want the messages that we share, we want them to have, to engage you as you're thinking and seeking Jesus.

127
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:38,240
Also, we love the passage of Scripture that says the number one command, the primary command for followers of God is to love the Lord your God with all of your heart, soul, mind, and strength.

128
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:40,700
That's kind of the holistic person, right?

129
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:44,200
But it's hard to preach sermons that are targeted at all of those things.

130
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:49,240
And so what we tend to do is we tend to target each one of those things in different series.

131
00:09:49,240 --> 00:09:54,860
So like our Practicing the Way series, it's very much about loving the Lord your God with all of your soul.

132
00:09:55,900 --> 00:10:01,240
And we touch on the other three aspects, but, you know, it's focused on one.

133
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:10,040
And so we try and vary the sermon series so that we're at least doing one sermon series a year on the head, on the heart, on the soul, on the hands, on the practical application.

134
00:10:10,040 --> 00:10:10,680
Does that make sense?

135
00:10:11,420 --> 00:10:15,300
So this series is going to be a bit, it's going to live a bit more in the head section.

136
00:10:15,300 --> 00:10:28,700
We're going to do a lot of kind of unpacking history and context of Paul's life and his ministry because we think it's important that we understand who Paul was since he was the writer of a majority of the New Testament.

137
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:29,860
Does that make sense?

138
00:10:29,860 --> 00:10:33,440
So this series on Paul, it's going to last the entire summer.

139
00:10:33,440 --> 00:10:38,040
It might seem like it's a long time, but I promise you it's not long enough.

140
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:45,160
We're kind of like caught in the middle between an in-depth study on the life of Paul and an overview.

141
00:10:46,440 --> 00:10:55,920
And so we're kind of living in that in-between, and I hope, I hope that this series will give you the necessary information you need to dive deeper into the life and ministry of Paul if you're curious.

142
00:10:55,920 --> 00:11:00,200
And if you're not, I hope that you get a lot out of it, and then you can forget that we ever did it.

143
00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:00,760
Does that make sense?

144
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:02,700
No, don't forget that we ever did it.

145
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:03,560
That would be missing the point.

146
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:06,160
All right.

147
00:11:06,160 --> 00:11:14,360
So some of you might be wondering, why do a 16-week series on the life of Paul instead of just looking at one of Paul's letters?

148
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:15,680
That's what normal churches do.

149
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:17,160
Right?

150
00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:17,640
Anybody?

151
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:18,100
No?

152
00:11:18,100 --> 00:11:18,440
Okay.

153
00:11:18,440 --> 00:11:22,200
Why not just do a series through one of the letters?

154
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:23,660
Well, I think that's a great question.

155
00:11:23,660 --> 00:11:31,920
And although we have done, and we probably will do in the future, a series through a letter of Paul, it would be deeply impactful.

156
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:39,120
But I think having a better understanding of who Paul is, his ministry career, his relationships with the churches that he's writing to,

157
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:45,700
I think it'll actually have a much more lasting impact on the way that we read the Scriptures, especially the New Testament.

158
00:11:45,860 --> 00:11:53,020
Again, Paul wrote 13 of 27 of the New Testament letters, and his letters make up one-third the pages of the New Testament.

159
00:11:53,800 --> 00:12:03,980
And so I think if we can understand who Paul is, understand his relationship to the churches, how he came into, what his mindset was as he became a leader, it'll help us better understand.

160
00:12:03,980 --> 00:12:10,380
The best illustration I could come up with, and I didn't check with Barrett on this.

161
00:12:10,440 --> 00:12:12,440
It's an art illustration, so hopefully it works.

162
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:17,680
But my thought is you could spend 16 weeks studying the Mona Lisa, right?

163
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:21,020
Looking at the Mona Lisa, trying to understand and appreciate the Mona Lisa.

164
00:12:21,240 --> 00:12:30,160
But if you spent 16 weeks kind of understanding Da Vinci, understanding his background, understanding how he learned to make art,

165
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:33,360
this is where my knowledge of Da Vinci ends.

166
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:39,140
But then you get all this background, all this appreciation, this understanding for how he was formed into an artist,

167
00:12:39,240 --> 00:12:46,160
and then you come to the Mona Lisa, it's like, oh, so much more is coming out, so much more is popping off the page.

168
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:57,120
So my hope is that as we kind of understand who Paul is, understand how God brought him to the place where he became the apostle that wrote 13 of the 27 New Testament letters,

169
00:12:57,120 --> 00:13:02,760
that then we go back to his letters, that it begins to kind of jump off the page to us in fresh, new ways.

170
00:13:02,760 --> 00:13:03,440
Does that make sense?

171
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:04,680
Okay.

172
00:13:04,680 --> 00:13:06,660
N.T. Wright says this.

173
00:13:06,660 --> 00:13:15,420
He says it is a safe bet to say that these letters, Paul's letters, page for page, have generated more comment, more sermons and seminars,

174
00:13:15,420 --> 00:13:19,900
more monographs and dissertations than any other writing from the ancient world.

175
00:13:19,900 --> 00:13:24,120
So I think if we can understand Paul better, it'll help us.

176
00:13:24,120 --> 00:13:30,080
And here's the tension that I have as a pastor and even just living in the American church.

177
00:13:31,420 --> 00:13:37,700
I think Christians today, we make one big error when we come to the Scriptures.

178
00:13:37,700 --> 00:13:40,400
And I don't know that we would articulate it out loud.

179
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:41,140
It happens to me.

180
00:13:41,140 --> 00:13:42,400
It probably happens to all of us.

181
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:50,640
I think oftentimes we treat the Bible as if God dropped the Bible down from heaven into our laps for us to read.

182
00:13:50,640 --> 00:13:54,080
Is anybody getting nervous?

183
00:13:54,080 --> 00:13:56,280
Because they're like, wait, that is what the Bible is, right?

184
00:13:58,280 --> 00:14:07,600
The Bible is actually, it's a collection of letters and prophecies, and it's written by human beings inspired by God.

185
00:14:07,600 --> 00:14:09,900
And it's written by those human beings to other human beings.

186
00:14:09,900 --> 00:14:13,440
And so essentially what we're doing when we're reading the Scriptures is we're reading somebody else's mail.

187
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:15,820
It was preserved for us.

188
00:14:15,820 --> 00:14:17,240
It was meant for us.

189
00:14:17,240 --> 00:14:18,260
We're supposed to read it.

190
00:14:18,260 --> 00:14:28,300
But if we read it as if God wrote it directly to us and not as if Paul wrote it to the Corinthians or Paul wrote it to the Colossians or Paul wrote it to the Ephesians, does that make sense?

191
00:14:28,300 --> 00:14:36,720
And I don't think many of us would articulate it out loud, but often I think we come to these texts and we think, okay, this is what God wants me to know about this thing.

192
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:45,900
And I think sometimes we actually end up missing what the text actually meant to the original recipients of the letters that Paul was writing.

193
00:14:45,900 --> 00:14:50,120
We talked about an example of this last week when we did communion.

194
00:14:50,120 --> 00:14:52,140
We did a celebratory communion.

195
00:14:53,260 --> 00:15:00,740
And we talked about there's a passage in 1 Corinthians where it kind of makes communion seem really serious and like really introspective.

196
00:15:00,740 --> 00:15:02,900
And you might die if you take this the wrong way.

197
00:15:02,900 --> 00:15:08,180
And I think what happens is Christians go to 1 Corinthians when we're prepping for communion.

198
00:15:08,180 --> 00:15:11,720
And we read it almost as if it's a textbook from God.

199
00:15:11,720 --> 00:15:14,820
And this one paragraph is all that God wants us to know about communion.

200
00:15:14,820 --> 00:15:18,740
And that would be reading the Scriptures incorrectly.

201
00:15:18,740 --> 00:15:20,040
Does that make sense?

202
00:15:20,600 --> 00:15:25,020
Because Paul was writing to a specific church who was doing communion incorrectly.

203
00:15:25,020 --> 00:15:28,220
And he was actually trying to correct their perspective on communion.

204
00:15:28,220 --> 00:15:32,880
And so if we take that paragraph and we say, this is dropped from heaven by God to me.

205
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:36,320
This is like a textbook for what he wants me to know about communion.

206
00:15:36,320 --> 00:15:37,080
It's not helpful.

207
00:15:37,080 --> 00:15:39,980
Because there's a lot more going on there.

208
00:15:39,980 --> 00:15:47,420
And so I think as we kind of dive deep into Paul's life, hopefully it'll help us better read the text of Scripture,

209
00:15:47,420 --> 00:15:50,140
which ultimately should be our aim as Christians.

210
00:15:50,280 --> 00:15:54,540
We should want to read and understand the text in the way that it was intended and apply it.

211
00:15:54,540 --> 00:15:55,120
Does that make sense?

212
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:56,700
Cool.

213
00:15:56,700 --> 00:15:58,800
So I think this series is going to be helpful in doing that.

214
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:03,320
Another reason I think this series is helpful and important, and in case you're wondering when I'm going to get to the sermon,

215
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:06,900
this is my elevator pitch for why you should pay attention to the next 16 weeks.

216
00:16:06,900 --> 00:16:08,500
And then we'll get to the sermon.

217
00:16:09,880 --> 00:16:16,280
Another reason I think it's really important is that Paul found himself in a really unique calling in the early church.

218
00:16:16,280 --> 00:16:21,040
He found himself between two very divided groups in the early church.

219
00:16:21,040 --> 00:16:23,260
Paul was as Jewish as you could be.

220
00:16:23,260 --> 00:16:24,540
We read about it during worship.

221
00:16:24,540 --> 00:16:26,360
He said, I am a Hebrew of Hebrews, right?

222
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:27,880
Circumcised on the eighth day.

223
00:16:27,880 --> 00:16:28,680
Tribe of Benjamin.

224
00:16:29,260 --> 00:16:33,980
Paul was as Jewish as you could be, and yet God called him to be an apostle to the Gentiles.

225
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:39,020
And in the early church, the Jews and the Gentiles were having a really hard time agreeing on stuff.

226
00:16:39,020 --> 00:16:41,680
They were having a really hard time finding unity.

227
00:16:41,680 --> 00:16:46,580
And because Paul's love and his passion and his relationship with the Jews,

228
00:16:46,580 --> 00:16:52,180
and yet his calling to the Gentiles, he found himself kind of being the bridge between the two groups,

229
00:16:52,180 --> 00:16:55,300
helping them to seek to find unity on what's most important,

230
00:16:55,300 --> 00:17:00,720
and then being able to kind of disagree on the things that aren't as important.

231
00:17:01,800 --> 00:17:07,580
And I don't know about you, but I feel like that is something that the church in America needs now more than ever.

232
00:17:07,580 --> 00:17:11,820
I think we can learn from Paul's perspective on unity,

233
00:17:11,820 --> 00:17:15,700
on bringing divided groups together for the sake of the kingdom of God.

234
00:17:15,700 --> 00:17:20,900
I think Paul's burden for unity in the church is a much-needed area of focus for us in the American church.

235
00:17:20,900 --> 00:17:24,420
And I think we can learn a lot from the life and ministry of Paul

236
00:17:24,420 --> 00:17:29,360
on how to unite for the most important things and agree to disagree on the less important things.

237
00:17:30,320 --> 00:17:34,820
All right, that's my elevator pitch for why you should pay attention for the next 16 weeks.

238
00:17:34,820 --> 00:17:36,320
Raise your hand if it convinced you.

239
00:17:36,320 --> 00:17:39,140
Okay, 85%.

240
00:17:39,140 --> 00:17:39,740
That's good.

241
00:17:39,740 --> 00:17:40,300
We'll take that.

242
00:17:40,300 --> 00:17:45,700
So here's kind of loose structure over the next 16 weeks.

243
00:17:45,700 --> 00:17:51,220
I really think Paul's life can be summarized in kind of five categories that we see.

244
00:17:51,220 --> 00:17:54,500
We're going to see today Paul as a persecutor.

245
00:17:54,500 --> 00:17:55,860
He actually didn't start as an apostle.

246
00:17:55,860 --> 00:17:57,180
He started persecuting the church.

247
00:17:57,620 --> 00:17:59,160
And then he became a missionary.

248
00:17:59,160 --> 00:18:03,980
And as he became a missionary, he then planted churches, which made him be a pastor.

249
00:18:03,980 --> 00:18:10,100
And then as the churches grew and as his kind of leadership grew in the churches,

250
00:18:10,100 --> 00:18:12,440
he essentially became a theologian to the churches.

251
00:18:12,440 --> 00:18:15,160
He started contributing to the theology of the churches.

252
00:18:15,160 --> 00:18:18,200
And then we'll see that he ends his life as a martyr.

253
00:18:18,320 --> 00:18:21,600
So over the next 16 weeks, we're going to look at a few of these categories.

254
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:25,180
It won't be super clear, but that's just the pattern of where we're headed.

255
00:18:25,180 --> 00:18:26,040
Sound good?

256
00:18:26,040 --> 00:18:27,760
Cool.

257
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:28,940
All right, we're going to pray.

258
00:18:28,940 --> 00:18:30,180
We need it.

259
00:18:31,100 --> 00:18:31,760
I need it.

260
00:18:31,760 --> 00:18:43,400
Dear Jesus, we're so grateful for the work you do in individuals' lives and the way that you use that work to impact others around them.

261
00:18:43,400 --> 00:18:51,880
God, we're so grateful for what you did in Paul's life and for the impact that he had on the church moving forward.

262
00:18:51,880 --> 00:19:00,600
And God, we just pray that we'd be able to play a small part in that, that we would be open and receptive to what you're doing in our life,

263
00:19:00,600 --> 00:19:04,700
that we'd be obedient and that through our obedience, you might use us to have an impact on those around us.

264
00:19:04,700 --> 00:19:06,900
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

265
00:19:06,900 --> 00:19:07,860
Amen.

266
00:19:07,860 --> 00:19:13,380
If you have your Bibles and you want to follow along, we're going to get to Acts chapter 7 in a little bit.

267
00:19:13,380 --> 00:19:18,780
We're going to get to Acts chapter 7 starting in verse 51, and we'll go through the beginning of 8.

268
00:19:19,400 --> 00:19:22,960
This is really the first time we come across Paul in the New Testament.

269
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:26,680
And like I said, he isn't Paul the apostle when we first see him in Scripture.

270
00:19:26,680 --> 00:19:28,780
He is Saul the persecutor.

271
00:19:28,780 --> 00:19:34,340
And again, just from the outset, what an amazing testimony of God's faithfulness in people's lives.

272
00:19:34,340 --> 00:19:36,220
This is what God does.

273
00:19:36,220 --> 00:19:41,820
God takes people who are enemies of him, and he transforms them into servants into his kingdom.

274
00:19:41,820 --> 00:19:44,960
God is still doing this to this very day.

275
00:19:46,020 --> 00:19:53,260
And as we're going to see next week, have you ever found yourself interacting with the Scriptures

276
00:19:53,260 --> 00:19:56,620
and realizing that you thought something that was totally wrong?

277
00:19:56,620 --> 00:19:58,240
Or am I the only one?

278
00:19:58,240 --> 00:19:59,620
Okay, some of us.

279
00:19:59,620 --> 00:20:02,420
Marissa and I, when we were in Bible college, Bible college people,

280
00:20:02,420 --> 00:20:04,840
like I went to a college that taught me the Bible.

281
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:12,180
And right when we were at Bible college, the History Channel came out with this like documentary series on the Bible.

282
00:20:12,180 --> 00:20:17,700
And they were doing these like vivid, you know, it was kind of the chosen before the chosen.

283
00:20:17,700 --> 00:20:19,180
And it was cool.

284
00:20:19,180 --> 00:20:22,000
And we were watching it one day, and they were doing the story of Samson.

285
00:20:22,740 --> 00:20:27,760
And, you know, the History Channel's got somewhat of a more liberal, secular perspective than I probably have towards the Scriptures.

286
00:20:27,760 --> 00:20:29,200
And so I'm like a little bit skeptical.

287
00:20:29,200 --> 00:20:33,620
And something's going on in Samson's life, and I'm like, that didn't happen.

288
00:20:33,620 --> 00:20:36,500
Are they just making stuff up?

289
00:20:36,500 --> 00:20:42,800
So I'm like looking at Marissa, and we're watching together, and she's like, no, that definitely didn't happen.

290
00:20:44,220 --> 00:20:48,860
So I get out my Bible, and I go to the story of Samson, and it happened.

291
00:20:48,860 --> 00:20:52,700
It totally happened, and I didn't remember it.

292
00:20:52,700 --> 00:20:56,940
And I think, I call this Sunday school theology.

293
00:20:56,940 --> 00:21:00,680
Like we learn things in Sunday school, and we kind of internalize lessons.

294
00:21:00,680 --> 00:21:06,980
And sometimes our sweet Sunday school teachers taught us something that maybe was correct, maybe was incorrect, maybe was somewhere in between.

295
00:21:07,800 --> 00:21:20,980
So, I say all that to say, some of you probably think, you have in your mind, that Saul had an encounter with Jesus on the road to Damascus, and Jesus changed Saul's name to Paul.

296
00:21:20,980 --> 00:21:21,760
Who thinks that?

297
00:21:21,760 --> 00:21:24,800
A couple of us, right?

298
00:21:24,800 --> 00:21:25,180
Yeah, yeah.

299
00:21:25,180 --> 00:21:26,180
Like, don't be ashamed.

300
00:21:26,180 --> 00:21:27,040
Like, that's fine.

301
00:21:27,040 --> 00:21:28,740
It's like a lot of us learn this.

302
00:21:28,740 --> 00:21:33,520
And the reality is that Paul was actually probably part of Saul's birth name, part of his full name.

303
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:44,840
And he ultimately started using it later in the book of Acts as he began to engage with the Gentile church more often, because Paul was more of a Gentile version of his name, more of the Roman influence.

304
00:21:44,840 --> 00:21:51,440
And so I say all that to say, I'm probably just going to say Saul and Paul backwards and forwards this whole time.

305
00:21:51,440 --> 00:21:53,320
And it's not because I'm getting it wrong.

306
00:21:53,320 --> 00:21:56,300
It's just because it's the same guy, okay?

307
00:21:56,300 --> 00:22:00,300
So, he did not have his name changed in an instant by Jesus.

308
00:22:00,740 --> 00:22:05,960
But you probably were taught that by your Sunday school teacher, or at least I was, and now we're all on the same page.

309
00:22:05,960 --> 00:22:12,860
So, Saul's introduction to the biblical narrative comes at the end of Acts 7.

310
00:22:12,860 --> 00:22:15,720
The Spirit of God has come upon the early church at Pentecost.

311
00:22:15,720 --> 00:22:20,680
The church is exploding in and around the city of Jerusalem, where the temple is.

312
00:22:20,680 --> 00:22:26,380
Thousands of Jews were turning to faith in Jesus as the one true Messiah of Israel.

313
00:22:26,380 --> 00:22:29,000
They were being baptized into his community of faith.

314
00:22:29,360 --> 00:22:35,720
These Jews were expressing, they were saying that the most Jewish thing that we can do is place our faith in Jesus.

315
00:22:35,720 --> 00:22:37,460
That he is Israel's Messiah.

316
00:22:37,460 --> 00:22:40,080
This is being Jewish.

317
00:22:40,080 --> 00:22:46,260
Other Jews, especially the Pharisees, whom Saul was a part of, they were enraged.

318
00:22:46,260 --> 00:22:51,820
And they thought, how in the world could a man who was crucified by the Romans be Israel's Messiah?

319
00:22:51,820 --> 00:22:56,320
The whole point of Israel's Messiah is that he is supposed to come and deliver us from the Romans.

320
00:22:56,400 --> 00:22:59,060
So, if the Romans crucified him, that makes no sense.

321
00:22:59,060 --> 00:23:00,240
He cannot be the Messiah.

322
00:23:00,240 --> 00:23:06,500
And you guys are leading people astray from what we should truly be doing as followers of Yahweh.

323
00:23:08,780 --> 00:23:12,240
And they would have scriptural backing to prove this.

324
00:23:12,240 --> 00:23:16,720
They would quote the Scripture about that he who hanged on a tree is cursed.

325
00:23:16,720 --> 00:23:18,520
And they say, how could Messiah be cursed?

326
00:23:18,520 --> 00:23:21,520
And so, they were struggling with this tension.

327
00:23:21,520 --> 00:23:28,800
And Saul and the other Pharisees, they felt like it was their duty to squash the growth of the early church, to destroy it.

328
00:23:29,340 --> 00:23:33,900
Because the early church's growth was a false teaching that was getting out of hand.

329
00:23:33,900 --> 00:23:38,520
So, they targeted one of the primary leaders of the church named Stephen.

330
00:23:38,520 --> 00:23:44,820
And Stephen was pretty awesome, pretty gifted speaker.

331
00:23:44,820 --> 00:23:46,520
And they were debating him.

332
00:23:46,520 --> 00:23:48,180
And these Pharisees, they were brilliant, right?

333
00:23:48,180 --> 00:23:51,220
They knew the law better than anybody else knew the law, or at least they thought.

334
00:23:51,220 --> 00:23:55,060
They're debating Stephen, and they cannot beat him in a religious debate.

335
00:23:55,720 --> 00:23:56,600
And so, guess what they do?

336
00:23:56,600 --> 00:23:59,580
They have people make false accusations about him.

337
00:23:59,580 --> 00:24:05,400
They bring him before a religious court, make false accusations about him, so that they can have him killed.

338
00:24:05,400 --> 00:24:06,280
Is this sounding familiar?

339
00:24:06,280 --> 00:24:09,860
That's exactly what happened to Jesus.

340
00:24:09,860 --> 00:24:12,060
They couldn't beat him in an argument.

341
00:24:12,060 --> 00:24:12,900
They couldn't defeat him.

342
00:24:12,900 --> 00:24:14,880
And so, all right, we've got to get rid of this guy.

343
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:19,280
We'll make false accusations, and we will get rid of him.

344
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:22,160
So, they bring Stephen before a court, a religious court.

345
00:24:22,860 --> 00:24:27,760
And he gives around what we will see is the longest speech in all of the New Testament.

346
00:24:27,760 --> 00:24:29,140
It's around 1,200 words.

347
00:24:29,140 --> 00:24:33,080
And there's a reason why Luke left it in here.

348
00:24:33,080 --> 00:24:34,660
There's a reason why it's here.

349
00:24:34,660 --> 00:24:37,200
It's so long, but it's important to the early church growth.

350
00:24:37,200 --> 00:24:46,580
And honestly, I think, my opinion is that the Pharisees were probably a little bit impressed with how much they agreed with Stephen

351
00:24:46,580 --> 00:24:50,180
for, like, the first, I don't know, 1,100 words.

352
00:24:50,180 --> 00:24:56,000
And then we'll see the last 100 words is where he gets himself in trouble, which is where we're going to pick up.

353
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:56,360
Okay?

354
00:24:56,360 --> 00:24:57,900
Acts 7.

355
00:24:57,900 --> 00:25:04,500
This message is probably going to feel, to a lot of us, like an information dump.

356
00:25:05,020 --> 00:25:09,660
I tried my best to not make it that way, and it's already feeling that way 10 minutes in.

357
00:25:09,660 --> 00:25:11,980
So, yeah.

358
00:25:11,980 --> 00:25:13,020
We'll see what we can do.

359
00:25:13,020 --> 00:25:16,640
And for some of you, it's going to be review, plus some new information.

360
00:25:16,640 --> 00:25:18,040
For others, it's just all review.

361
00:25:18,040 --> 00:25:20,020
And for some of you, it's going to be all new information.

362
00:25:20,020 --> 00:25:22,280
So, we're just going to do our best.

363
00:25:22,900 --> 00:25:23,100
All right.

364
00:25:23,100 --> 00:25:24,260
Acts 7, 51.

365
00:25:24,260 --> 00:25:24,920
This is Stephen.

366
00:25:24,920 --> 00:25:29,360
This is his response to the religious leaders who have made false accusations about him.

367
00:25:29,360 --> 00:25:32,040
The last 100 words or so of his speech, he says,

368
00:25:32,040 --> 00:25:37,980
You stiff-necked people, uncircumcised in heart and ears, you always resist the Holy Spirit.

369
00:25:37,980 --> 00:25:40,300
As your father did, so do you.

370
00:25:40,300 --> 00:25:42,740
As your fathers did, so did you.

371
00:25:42,740 --> 00:25:45,700
Which of the prophets did your fathers not persecute?

372
00:25:45,700 --> 00:25:49,480
And they killed those who announced beforehand the coming of the righteous one,

373
00:25:49,480 --> 00:25:52,320
whom you have now betrayed and murdered.

374
00:25:53,640 --> 00:25:58,180
You who received the law as delivered by angels, and you did not keep it.

375
00:25:58,180 --> 00:26:03,520
Now, when they heard these things, they were enraged, and they ground their teeth at him.

376
00:26:03,520 --> 00:26:09,660
But he, full of the Holy Spirit, gazed into heaven and saw the glory of God and Jesus standing at the right hand of God.

377
00:26:09,660 --> 00:26:14,160
And he said, Behold, I see the heavens opened and the Son of Man standing at the right hand.

378
00:26:14,160 --> 00:26:16,660
They cried out with a loud voice.

379
00:26:16,660 --> 00:26:18,800
They stopped their teeth and rushed together at him.

380
00:26:18,800 --> 00:26:21,480
Then they cast him out of the city and stoned him.

381
00:26:22,680 --> 00:26:28,240
And the witnesses laid their garments at the feet of a young man named Saul.

382
00:26:29,960 --> 00:26:33,840
As they were stoning Stephen, he called out, Lord Jesus, receive my spirit.

383
00:26:33,840 --> 00:26:40,940
And falling to his knees, he cried out with a loud voice, Lord, do not hold the sin against them.

384
00:26:40,940 --> 00:26:44,080
When he had said this, he fell asleep.

385
00:26:45,380 --> 00:26:51,040
And Saul, this young man whom they laid the garments at his feet, he approved of his execution.

386
00:26:51,040 --> 00:26:56,060
And there arose on that day a great persecution against the church in Jerusalem.

387
00:26:56,060 --> 00:27:01,200
And they were all scattered throughout the regions of Judea, Samaria, except for the apostles.

388
00:27:01,200 --> 00:27:04,840
Devout men buried Stephen and made great lamentation over him.

389
00:27:04,900 --> 00:27:09,020
But Saul was ravaging the church and entering house after house.

390
00:27:09,020 --> 00:27:12,840
He dragged off men and women and committed them to prison.

391
00:27:15,320 --> 00:27:21,240
This is the introduction to the man who would write one-third of the New Testament.

392
00:27:21,240 --> 00:27:28,740
Enraged by a man who essentially is reenacting the crucifixion of Jesus right in front of him.

393
00:27:28,740 --> 00:27:37,180
A faithful follower of Jesus, following Christ's example unto death and laying down his life in front of his enemies

394
00:27:37,180 --> 00:27:43,420
in order to demonstrate the love of God to the Pharisees, to the religious leaders.

395
00:27:43,420 --> 00:27:49,540
And the reaction was fury, anger, enragement.

396
00:27:49,540 --> 00:27:50,020
How?

397
00:27:50,020 --> 00:27:51,400
How did he get there?

398
00:27:51,400 --> 00:27:57,440
How could Saul have found himself in this situation?

399
00:27:57,800 --> 00:28:03,920
I want to do my best to unpack some history and some of Saul's upbringing

400
00:28:03,920 --> 00:28:08,760
that might help us just kind of wrap our heads a little bit around how Saul could have gotten there.

401
00:28:08,760 --> 00:28:15,280
How he could have lived in this way in response to such a faithful follower of God in front of him.

402
00:28:15,280 --> 00:28:22,080
We know from the Scriptures that it says that Saul was a Pharisee, the son of Pharisees.

403
00:28:22,080 --> 00:28:26,780
And so Pharisee was a part of how Saul identified himself.

404
00:28:26,960 --> 00:28:28,640
It was in his core.

405
00:28:28,640 --> 00:28:31,560
Saul had come from a long lineage of Pharisees.

406
00:28:31,560 --> 00:28:35,080
It wasn't just a part of his education or his ideology.

407
00:28:35,080 --> 00:28:39,040
It was a key component of what made up his identity.

408
00:28:40,140 --> 00:28:46,420
So I think we need to think about the Pharisees similar to denomination.

409
00:28:46,420 --> 00:28:57,560
It's a religious ideology, but with much more passion and zeal and intensity to an allegiance to their ideology.

410
00:28:57,560 --> 00:29:02,440
So, anybody ever met somebody from Texas?

411
00:29:02,440 --> 00:29:07,720
How long does it take them to tell you that they're from Texas?

412
00:29:07,720 --> 00:29:11,380
I'm looking at Helen and Chris.

413
00:29:11,380 --> 00:29:13,480
How long does it take you, right?

414
00:29:13,800 --> 00:29:20,180
And then after they tell you that they're from Texas, how much longer does it take them to tell you how much better Texas is than wherever you are?

415
00:29:20,180 --> 00:29:31,220
So Pharisees were like, they were like a Jewish denomination with this certainty of those who live in Texas.

416
00:29:31,480 --> 00:29:40,040
This identity, it's like this is how we identify ourselves by our belief system and how we respond to the law of God.

417
00:29:41,020 --> 00:29:46,980
So, just for even more historical context, again, this feels like a lecture, and I'm sorry.

418
00:29:46,980 --> 00:29:48,460
I promise I'll preach at some point.

419
00:29:48,460 --> 00:29:49,820
It's important.

420
00:29:49,820 --> 00:29:53,820
The Jews, they were promised the promised land.

421
00:29:53,820 --> 00:29:58,620
They came into the promised land, but then they were disobedient, unfaithful to God's promises.

422
00:29:58,620 --> 00:30:00,940
And God said, you're going to go into exile.

423
00:30:00,940 --> 00:30:04,720
The Babylonians and the Assyrians are going to take you into exile, but don't worry.

424
00:30:04,720 --> 00:30:06,660
I will bring you back to the land.

425
00:30:07,980 --> 00:30:12,000
And so, this happened exactly like God said it was going to happen.

426
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:15,100
And they're exiled.

427
00:30:15,100 --> 00:30:21,440
Many of the Jews have made their way back to the promised land, but yet it had not lived up to their prophetic expectations.

428
00:30:21,440 --> 00:30:24,340
They were not back in charge of the land.

429
00:30:24,340 --> 00:30:26,860
They were still under Roman oppression.

430
00:30:26,860 --> 00:30:28,300
And not all the Jews were back.

431
00:30:28,300 --> 00:30:29,700
Only some of the Jews were back.

432
00:30:29,700 --> 00:30:32,220
And so, they're still waiting for this Messiah.

433
00:30:32,220 --> 00:30:36,880
And they're kind of in this in-between stage where they have to decide how they're going to interact with God's law.

434
00:30:37,740 --> 00:30:40,200
And this is where the five denominations come up.

435
00:30:40,200 --> 00:30:40,860
The five groups.

436
00:30:40,860 --> 00:30:45,920
The five responses to the situation that they find themselves in.

437
00:30:45,920 --> 00:30:49,460
Pharisee is one of those five responses.

438
00:30:49,460 --> 00:30:51,860
Jews both in Jerusalem.

439
00:30:51,860 --> 00:30:54,420
And I think I'm going to pronounce this right.

440
00:30:54,420 --> 00:30:54,940
I hope so.

441
00:30:54,940 --> 00:30:56,800
The diaspora.

442
00:30:56,800 --> 00:30:58,520
Look at your neighbor and say diaspora.

443
00:30:58,520 --> 00:31:00,100
Yeah.

444
00:31:00,100 --> 00:31:01,220
That's awesome.

